From c3908a8703f20b4fdf97292adccd84a654cc4305 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Shammah Chancellor <shammah.chancellor@notvalid.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 05:10:40 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 07/30] test: discover the functional tests by importing their
 modules

New test_framework/discovery.py finds the test classes in the selected
test files: concrete subclasses of BitcoinTestFramework defined in the
file itself.
- Classes imported from another test file count only where they are
  defined.
- Classes with `abstract = True` are skipped.
- num_nodes and setup_clean_chain are read when they are class
  attributes.
- A file that cannot be imported, or defines no test, gives a spec
  carrying the error.

Importing a test module can have side effects, and every module needs
SRCDIR to import (test_framework/cdefs.py). So test_runner.py runs
discovery in a separate process with SRCDIR set:
    python3 -m test_framework.discovery <tests_dir> <files...>
Importing all ~290 test modules takes about half a second.

The runner now fails a file that cannot be imported, with the error, at
once and without starting it (e.g. chronik_electrum_basic.py when the
Python websocket module is missing: same result as before, without
running a process). The discovered classes will let the runner run
tests in its own worker processes.

Also: the test framework's in-process unit tests no longer leak their
log lines (TestFramework, asyncio) into the runner's output.

Unit tests cover mixins, an abstract base, an imported test class, an
import error and a file without tests. The full functional suite gives
the same per-test results as before.
